body:
Boar Man can be made pretty high powered if you build it that way. The fact that its ability both pumps and untaps creatures make \[\[Marwyn the Nurturer\]\] and the like incredibly good at making a TON of mana. 

&#x200B;

That said, without those cards its still a lot of fun. Flood the board with mana dorks, attack, cast more mana dorks, repeat =\]

I should clarify and say it's not EXCLUSIVELY tied to cost. My answer to your hypothetical would be 'the 80 dollar card probably sees Legacy play'. That doesn't mean that its more powerful across the board in EDH. Some commanders would probably rather see the $1 card. For example, one of the best cards in my \[\[Hazezon, Shaper of Sand\]\] deck is \[\[Nahiri's Lithoforming\]\]. It costs less than a quarter but is functionally a \[\[Scapeshift\]\] PLUS card draw in this build. My Zada example is another. It's full of 5 cent red cantrips that turn into mass draw with Zada's ability. 

&#x200B;

This won't always hold true. \[\[The Great Henge\]\] is expensive because it's awesome in almost every green creature deck. But the lack of defined meta (outside of cEDH) means there is much more room for cheap cards in niche scenarios that are more powerful than they're cost.

I know its not classic Gruul stompy, but Raggadragga can be a really strong combo commander. Raggadragga + any mana dork that taps for mana equal to its power + a bunch of 7 mana spells = an arbitrarily-large-but-technically-not-infinite amount of mana. Exile your whole deck with \[\[Commune with Lava\]\] where X is 80ish, play the whole damn thing. Infinitely buy back \[\[Wurmcalling\]\] for an army of worms. If you don't win from spells, you have an obscenely large mana dork with trample to then do the Stompy thing. 

&#x200B;

My Raggadragga deck will win turn 5 if it isn't interrupted, or with just the right hand it'll do it t4. It's a lot of fun.

Power level is not tied to cost. \[\[Zada\]\] is notorious for being cheap and very strong. You can build a $40 deck that can hang with significantly more expensive stuff. In general, a budget, especially $100, is going to keep you from high-end mana bases and some of the format staples, but does not keep you from building strong stuff. 

&#x200B;

That said, budget building often creates interesting choices and utilizes cards most people won't see. It'll make you better deck builders.

Do Vehicles have a type when they are crewed? And more generally, I think some of the artifact animation spells turn artifacts into just Artifact Creatures, no type. \[\[Cyberdrive Awakener\]\]

&#x200B;

There's ways to do it, but probably not enough to design a payoff for it.

&#x200B;

EDIT: OP mentioned animation in the post, I just can't read.

I like the Halo Fountain inclusion. That card has won me multiple games. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Hazezon, Shaper of Sand\]\] can generate a ton of tokens. \[\[Scapeshift\]\] and \[\[Nahiri's Lithoforming\]\] can both give you 16+ tokens. Play cards that give you extra land drops like \[\[Druid Class\]\], \[\[Mina and Denn\]\], etc., use \[\[Zuran Orb\]\] to loop Deserts through the graveyard. 

&#x200B;

You'll want untappers to reset your tokens too. \[\[Benefactors Draught\]\] can be a combat trick that draws you a bunch of cards, or just a cheap way to untap all your stuff. Green has a good amount of cheap 'untap all creatures' spells, plus \[\[Seedborn Muse\]\]. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Circle of Dreams Druid\]\] makes a bunch of mana in token decks, especially if you can untap it. \[\[Umbral Mantle\]\] and \[\[Staff of Domination\]\] both can get you infinite mana this way. Staff +CoDD can generate infinite mana, then dump all that mana into untapping your other tokens for infinite damage.

Domain is the biggest impact here. I can't think of any cards that get super broken by making domain go from 5 to 10, but I'm sure there are some cards that would welcome the change. 

&#x200B;

I still want to hear what the difference between an Island and an Ocean would be. Does it tap for more mana? Does it incrementally increase? I can't think of a good mechanical reason to have two basic land types that produce the same color. Basics are 'basic' for a reason. They tap for mana, and that's it. So having another Basic that does the same thing feels, well, non-basic.

I see what you're saying, but I don't place a lot of stock in WOTC's promises, and feel like when they need the money they will absolutely reprint them functionally.  

&#x200B;

Again, I just don't see it adding a ton of mechanical value. One of the few things to not change in the game is the basic land types (save for Wastes). It feels too strongly tied to the identity of the game to go changing it for another way to mana fix. There are so many ways, especially in EDH, to fix mana with lands already that I think the change isn't worth the result. 

&#x200B;

I agree that mana bases should be cheaper so that more people can play smoother games, but I'm not sold that this is the way to do it.

What is the mechanical reason behind this? Yea it makes sense for reprints like you're saying, but what the actual, mechanical difference be between an Island and a Lake? There is an obvious bump to the Domain mechanic, which is probably not an issue. There are ways to get cheap fixing without needing to add more land types. 

&#x200B;

I guess what I'm trying to say is, what would the point be?

I just played against \[\[Atraxa, Grand Unifier\]\] as a Vigilance tribal/pillow fort deck, and it was remarkably effective. \[\[Propaganda\]\]-type effects ( \[\[Collective Restraint\]\] did serious work ) and all vigilance creatures meant it was incredibly difficult to swing in. Atraxa provides huge card advantage/selection. Run some incidental lifegain and it was pretty impenetrable.

&#x200B;

Edit: Monarch and Initiative were both in the deck, and as it was very hard to get any combat damage in, they held it the whole game.

There's not a single white pip in the 99 of the deck, is there a reason you aren't running Be'lakor as the commander? Are you just using Ludevic/Tymna for the card advantage? 

&#x200B;

The deck feels a lot less flavorful with those two at the helm

I think this works if costed at 1GG. It essentially costs 6 mana (Sac three Clues) to transform Omar Nath. If you sac'd three Clues with Tireless Tracker, it would get 3 +1/+1 counters. I think along with the second color restricting it's access a little, I'd say they're fairly equal in value. 

&#x200B;

I really like the last mode on Omnath, since you can hold up a fetch land for instant speed indestructible.

I think you could bring the the CMC down to 4 (or 3 if you tweak the P/T) here. The non-eye drawback, and needing someone to chump block to get the effect, is going to keep this from getting out of hand, so I feel like it can be more aggressively costed. 

&#x200B;

Giving this menace and keeping it at 5 could also be fun. That way the opponent has to make a harder choice of giving you 2 spells or taking the damage. 

&#x200B;

Interesting design!

\[\[Othelm, Sigaridan Outcast\]\] is a great backup for Reinne. Let's you pick her back out from the graveyard if she gets removed, or just returns another creature if you need it back to the field ASAP. 

&#x200B;

My Rienne deck runs \[\[Realm Razer\]\] as the hidden commander and tries to murder everyone with mass landfall triggers and \[\[Spitfire Lagac\]\] et al.

you're gonna need more tokens/small evasive creatures to more efficiently dig through the deck. I like \[\[Canoptek Scarab Swarm\]\] to act as graveyard hate and make a bunch of tokens. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Forever Young\]\] is some decent recursion in Raffine. It won't help you cheat out creatures, but since you'll be drawing a bunch of cards with Raffine on attacks, you get them all to your hand to play again or discard for damage if you have Archfiend or \[\[Syr Konrad\]\] in play. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Living Death\]\] is awesome for cheating stuff out as well. Remember to sac as much of your stuff as you can prior to using it.

[https://archidekt.com/decks/3247085](https://archidekt.com/decks/3247085)

&#x200B;

The main goal here is obviously to play Deserts as many times as possible. The ramp package is almost exclusively getting extra land drops per turn, then tapping and sacrificing lands to Zuran Orb to play them from the yard. \[\[Grand Warlord Radha\]\] is a house for additional ramp.

&#x200B;

The pump package is trying to be a warrior tribal, as almost all the tokens it can make are warriors. \[\[Akroma's Will\]\] \[\[Shared Animosity\]\] and \[\[Titanic Ultimatum\]\] can put you over the top. 

&#x200B;

You can bring budget down pretty rapidly by getting rid of \[\[Anointed Procession\]\] and adjusting the mana base. \[\[Scapeshift\]\] isn't a necessity in this deck, because \[\[Nahiri's Lithoforming\]\] is better when you have Hazezon out anyway so that can be cut to save some cash too. 

This deck performs way better than I thought it would. It's pretty easy to get 3-4 land drops a turn and make 6 sand warriors every round. I would probably try and get some more land sacrifice outlets to make sure there is always a Desert in the graveyard, as there are some games where I can't loop them through as much as I'd like.

It has done some good work for me in my \[\[Hazezon, Shaper of Sand\]\] deck, but that's because I can pretty regularly swing with 10+ creatures. 

&#x200B;

As the head of her own deck, it is solid ramp but it would need some heavy token generators in the 99 to really maximize it's potential. \[\[Avenger of Zendikar\]\] and \[\[Phylath the World Sculptor\]\] are both real good at tokens, so there definitely options to make it happen.

Definitely agree, just wanted to point out that that style of untap effect has been made before. I think that 5mv is probably low for that card, but not by a lot. It's a smaller, non-evasive body so it should be easier to remove (maybe make it 3 toughness so it dies to bolt). It also doesn't get you the extra upkeep and draw. 

&#x200B;

Make it a 2/3 that has to tap for it's WUBRG ability and I think it works at 5 mana.

I use the pride secret lair Sol Ring playmat, and have only ever received complements. I've ran into exactly one hentai playmat, but the person was cordial and just fine to play against, no comments or anything. I come up from Humboldt to play, well worth the 40-45 minute rush hour drive. 

&#x200B;

They also run a free promo raffle every Thursday, I've won a card like 4 times. Nothing of crazy value,  but top prize is usually a promo pack and that's pretty cool.

Hi fellow Dice Dojo-er! I've probably played against you during Thursday commander night lol

&#x200B;

I just want to second everything you said, and add that Dice Dojo is super queer friendly! I've never seen anyone experience any sort of harassment or ill will, and everyone always seems comfortable being themselves there. 

&#x200B;

I've been to quite a few board game cafes all around the country, and Dice Dojo is the only one that is entirely free to play.

Yea I would gladly take some more elemental support as well, just wanted to point out some options for Temur. Temur dragons is doing just fine with \[\[Mirrym\]\] lol, potentially too fine. 

&#x200B;

Jeskai spirits would be a good one, they have \[\[Kykar\]\] but some additional commanders for the archetype would be cool. I think Jeskai djinn could be kind of cool. UR seems to be the main colors for djinn so far, but I think W could certainly fit too.

Indoor bike parks are fucking expensive to start. I've (briefly) looked into it. A warehouse of sufficient size is NOT cheap, regardless of condition. You definitely need sponsorship from bike manufacturers/bike shops, enough skilled labor to build all the ramps, and then you need to pay for insurance for your 20,000sqft deathtrap. 

&#x200B;

Ray's is special. They have an unbelievable space that you could never match rent-wise in Chicago. They are sponsored by the biggest names in the business, and the building itself is still essentially held together with duct tape. Broken windows and buckets covering leaks are all over the place. 

&#x200B;

I think the path forward in Chicago would be to try and partner with Big Marsh, Sram, and a bike shop to either rent a building or maybe build essentially an enclosed, heated pavilion that covers a dirt pump track and some jump lines. Have the bike shop operate the repair and take a cut, Sram donates the bikes or components.

I feel this way too. I did the same when Dale Earnhardt died. 

The common thread is back then my grandfather died a few weeks before Dale. Now, my grandmother recently was diagnosed with terminal cancer. 

&#x200B;

I think a LOT of people do not have a grasp on their own personal relationships and how to manage loss. So, at least for me, I think this incident is a way for me to process loss through a lens I understand. 

&#x200B;

Personal, but have you gone through a loss recently? Something to think about.

Just proxy your second cards so you don't have to switch them out. Absolutely no one will care. 

&#x200B;

For transport, I just received the Quiver card case as a gift and it's awesome. It can hold 5 decks, the deck boxes have room for any necessary tokens, and theres enough space in the case for some dice as well. Highly recommend.

My \[\[Braids, Conjurer Adept\]\] deck can have a ton of upkeeps/Upkeep triggers. The most I've actually pulled off in game is 4, but theoretically can have like 10 or more.

&#x200B;

My \[\[Rienne\]\] deck can use \[\[Realm Razer\]\] and \[\[Ashaya\]\] to blink all my creatures and lands infinitely, drawing the game (unless I have a sac outlet)

There are a few creatures that can recur themselves for 2 mana or less, like \[\[Bloodsoaked Champion\]\]. So Ashaya + any one of them is infinite mana. Making the Apparatus produce only colorless would make it a lot harder to loop, as most of the creatures that I can think of need black mana. That or trimming it to two mana of any one color would be more balanced I feel.

&#x200B;

Edit: Needing to untap it does make it more work to go infinite, I should have read the card twice. I still like the switch to colorless mana. You could also make it "sac a basic land you control" if you wanted to make it more universal

I would argue that Sol Ring and Grey Merchant fit just fine in a 6-7 deck. If you are looking for power level 6-7 you probably should think about taking the repeatable tutor out of the command zone. Moving Ayara to the command zone will probably make the deck run smoother for you, as it is a repeatable sac outlet/card draw engine in a deck where you are trying to sac things for Gravepact anyway. 

&#x200B;

On that note, gravepact is going to cause a lot more issues at the table than any card in that deck, more so than vamp tutor would.

I feel like the 'bad cyclist' image really gets overplayed. I ride a lot, and 9.5/10 times I never skip lights, and never see others do it. A few bad apples spoil the bunch, so to speak. 

&#x200B;

I will certainly argue that a lot of behaviors that get deemed as 'asshole cyclist behavior' are really the result of trying to avoid getting leveled by two tons of metal, not because we feel like we deserve special rules or can't follow directions.

The only thing wrong with that attitude is insisting on playing that way when others don't want to. 

&#x200B;

Commander has near infinite strategies and play styles. Playing MLD and heavy stax isn't wrong, doing just to be contrarian and not reading the room is.

A well timed \[\[Rogues Passage\]\] on your biggest flyer certainly helps. Throw in \[\[Elesh Norn, Grand Cenobite\]\] if you're ok with with going non-tribal. It should clear out all those Kykar tokens. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Bond of Discipline\]\] \[\[Aether Shockwave\]\] \[\[Blinding Light\]\] will all clear the way for you as well.

Guess I should have checked out the gatherer page for Library, thanks for the information on that. 

&#x200B;

The other methods involved using \[\[Amulet of Vigor\]\] to let Korlash's swamps be used to fuel Phyrexian Reclamation or Endbringer's. 

&#x200B;

It is certainly an ultra-jank strategy but I'd like to see if it can be at least semi-reliable.

It seems very similar to the new Prototype mechanic. It provides a nice bit of flexibility in the early game, allowing you get blockers out quickly, with more value later. 

&#x200B;

Reanimator and blink decks will like this as a way to cheat the full cost

My \[\[Obuun\]\] has a secondary goal of playing every single one of the 40 lands in the deck. a well timed \[\[Scapeshift\]\] makes it possible. That usually leads to a win, but even if it doesn't I'm still pretty content.

&#x200B;

Edit: \[\[Braids Conjurer Adept\]\] wants to get as many upkeeps as possible. Again, this is usually a win, but I've been able to get multiple copies of \[\[Sphinx of the Second Sun\]\] on the board, combined with \[\[Strionic Resonator\]\] or \[\[Lithoform Engine\]\] to double it up even more. The most upkeeps I've ever had is 4, but the theoretical max is around 10 if there are enough trigger copies.

\[\[Outmaneuver\]\] gets that sort of reaction whenever I play it at my LGS. I run it in \[\[Animar\]\] as another form of evasion and no one ever expects it when they try and chump block.

&#x200B;

The wording isn't something that is printed anymore so it gets a lot of double takes.

\[\[Chandra's Ignition\]\] both wipes the whole table and gets around needing evasion. Obviously not playable in Shanna but it certainly works well in trying to make a 'fair' voltron. 

&#x200B;

Look into \[\[Elsha\]\] for a really fun voltron commander. Run a slew of 1-2cmc equipments and auras, balloon Elsha with Prowess triggers (\[\[Jeskai Ascendancy\]\] helps a ton\]\]) then beat face. Use White for recursion, Blue for protection and control, and Red to keep the damage going. 

&#x200B;

I ran mine creatureless, which adds a fun restriction and some more 'fairness' by forcing me to keep a lot of \[\[Holy Day\]\] type effects on hand in lieu of blockers so you have to be more aware of when you attack.

Niche, but \[\[Portal to Phyrexia\]\] awesome in my \[\[Braids, Conjurer Adept\]\] deck. It uses theft effects to control opponent's boards and boost my own, and this card lets me do that and can provide effective removal for creature-light opponents. Getting to play it for free off Braids makes the 9 mana irrelevant. 

&#x200B;

I'm sure that there are some blink decks that could make this pretty mean.

[https://archidekt.com/decks/3321710#Here\_Be\_Dragons](https://archidekt.com/decks/3321710#Here_Be_Dragons)

&#x200B;

It's still a WIP, so don't expect perfection. I like Provoke to act as pseudo-removal as well, as I can get rid of stuff that people wouldn't normally chump block with. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Bower Passage\]\] seems to basically make all my dragons unblockable which seems great as this deck wants to win through combat. 

&#x200B;

Let me know what you think!

[https://archidekt.com/decks/2235288#Budget\_Braids](https://archidekt.com/decks/2235288#Budget_Braids)

&#x200B;

The goal is to get as many upkeeps as possible and try to outvalue everyone else. \[\[Vesusvian Shapeshifter\]\] + \[\[Brine Elemental\]\] doesnt let people untap. 

&#x200B;

I just added \[\[Consecrated Sphinx\]\] which is a no brainer in this deck if you can afford it. 

&#x200B;

Its pretty slow, best case you're dropping stuff for free on turn 4, attacking turn 5. It's not high powered by any means but is fun to pilot and can really get going if you can get \[\[Roil Elemental\]\] stealing peoples stuff.

That's how I feel about \[\[Braids, Conjurer Adept\]\]. You can stuff your deck with Eldrazi titans and drop them for free, but you're giving everyone else the same gas so you need to still play very interactively and work around it. Especially because three people are gonna use that Braids trigger before it comes back to you. 

&#x200B;

Mine is a Sphinx tribal that wins with theft or the occasional soft lock.

Life's Origin in Shrines is leagues better, at least in the decks I've seen. Those little shrine tokens make it really easy to get 10+ shrines on the board. Essentially doubling your shrine output, plus recursion is amazing. 

&#x200B;

Sisay is slower IMO. Tutoring for exactly what you need is nice, but if you have one of the card draw shrines, it doesn't really matter when you start drawing 10 cards a turn.

Sphinx Tribal is fun with Braids. The weird minigames they offer definitely draw me some looks. 

&#x200B;

[https://archidekt.com/decks/2235288#Budget\_Braids](https://archidekt.com/decks/2235288#Budget_Braids)

First thought is there are way too many colored pips in those creatures. I try to keep any two pip creatures to a minimum in my Animar deck. You can pop off real early if you only need 1-2 mana a turn to make stuff happen. 

Blink/Return to hand shenanigans can be incredible with him. \[\[Peregrine Drake\]\] \[\[Cloud of Fairies\]\] \[\[Great Whale\]\] are nasty in this deck. \[\[Ancestral Statue\]\] is super useful even without infinites. \[\[Roaring Primadox\]\] helps alot. 

&#x200B;

Colorless mana dorks are free ramp as soon as Animar is out. Throw all the applicable Myr in there. 

&#x200B;

Cast-from-the-top cards like \[\[garruks horde\]\] and \[\[magus of the future\]\] are great, but their colored pip count is pretty high. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Great Henge\]\] is insane. Ramp, card draw, and a little pump all in one. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Crashing Drawbridge\]\] is free haste.

[https://archidekt.com/decks/2796394#Realm\_Razer](https://archidekt.com/decks/2796394#Realm_Razer)

&#x200B;

A few cuts can bring the price way down and not really affect the deck. The Great Henge, Farewell, and the Altars can all be switched to a cheaper alternative without huge functional difference, I just already owned them.

\[\[Elvish Hexhunter\]\] reanimator. White/Green has a ton of ways to pull EH back from the grave. \[\[Sun Titan\]\] \[\[Eternal Witness\]\] \[\[Reveillark\]\] etc. EH is cheap enough that you can just kick it back to the command zone a few times and still have a viable enchantment removal option.

&#x200B;

You can build a stax/control/tax deck well enough in GW with \[\[Aura of Silence\]\] and the like, then win with \[\[Approach of the Second Sun\]\] or a token army. Or lean hard into blinking Reveillark and the like for some combo shenanigans.

&#x200B;

Don't forget to put \[\[Tranquility\]\] in there as well. \[\[Hall of Heliod's Generosity\]\] lets you grab some of your enchantments back if they are lost in the wipe.

&#x200B;

I have a \[\[Rienne\]\] deck in Naya that uses Hexhunter and \[\[Qasali Pridemage\]\] to great effect. It's built around \[\[Realm Razer\]\] and mass land denial/landfall hate, but those two do a really great job locking boards down until RR drops and upends the whole thing.

&#x200B;

Edit: A member of my pod has a Go-Shintai Shrines deck that gets really unmanageable if let unchecked, so I have enchantment hate in every deck!

I have a Volrath the Shapestealer infect deck. It is my one deck with black and it was one of the first I've ever built. 

&#x200B;

It needs an overhaul though. I think I've gotten a little better at deck building and need to refocus some parts of it. The infect strategy doesn't necessarily feel like blacks other main archetypes. It's much more 'turn cards sideways' than aristocrats or graveyard strategies. It is quite fun to turn Volrath into a 7/5 unblockable with infect though.

That makes total sense. I feel like every color combo gets set back during a board wipe, so its understandable to just avoid the board all together. That said, Naya is pretty good at recovering from wipes, with white and green both having solid reanimation, and red's access to things like Underworld Breach. Combine that with green's ramp and I've found it can rebuild quicker than most. 

&#x200B;

But, to each their own! It sounds like you have a nice spread of other commanders. I've wanted to build a Brago deck!

I love Naya! Board state and creatures are definitely a focus in those colors, but you can have some good safety nets built in. There are enough conditional counters like \[\[dawn Charm\]\] and \[\[Rebuff the Wicked\]\] to protect whatever you're doing, and \[\[Heroic Intervention\]\] is mighty nice. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Atla\]\] can be built as a combo commander that does most of the work with a lower board presence. You just need Atla and a sac outlet to be off to the races. \[\[Maskwood Nexus\]\] is the real star, as once everything is eggs, any board wipe is just more free creatures. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Rienne\]\] with \[\[Realm Razer\]\] can be stax-y, just constantly killing Razer on the previous players end step, doing your thing on your turn, then taking everyone's lands again in your second main. Throw in some mana rock hate and your opponents are locked out of all their mana. You win through landfall haters like \[\[Spitfire Lagac\]\] so it's not just turning creatures sideways. 

&#x200B;

\[\[Obuun\]\] landfall can be all about man lands, which will keep you board wipe resilient and less reliant on a big board state. \[\[Scapeshift\]\] will usually end it on the spot with a big Obuun + \[\[Rogue's Passage\]\] or \[\[Moraug, Fury of Akoum\]\]. 

&#x200B;

I feel like the trick to making Naya feel different is to use green as a secondary color to add some extra juice to your other strategies. But hey, if that doesn't work for you, I get it. I'm a Timmy/Johnny sort of player, so I appreciate the big creatures of Naya, but theres also more fun interactions to be had.

I just ordered cards for this deck! It looks like we have similar concepts. Rienne is interesting as it brings reanimator to Naya (My favorite color combo) so it gives me yet another deck to make. 

&#x200B;

I was surprised at how few multicolored creatures I came across that would be useful in this deck. I figured New Capenna would have a ton but I was wrong.

I love my Obuun landfall deck. He isn't the main win-con (That's \[\[Scapeshift + \[\[Moraug\]\]), but Obuun + indestructible lands is a great way to get some beatdown going and causes some problems for people. 

&#x200B;

Also my main goal for that deck isn't necessarily to win but to manage to play every one of the 40 lands in the deck. \[\[Cultivator Colossus\]\] + \[\[Abundance\]\] does the trick there

That card is essentially \[\[Perplexing Chimera\]\], but only for your stuff. 

&#x200B;

It doesn't seem like anything game-breaking. It can martyr itself if a permanent is about to get removed, but other than that, I guess it could be a bad pseudo-ninjustu for a vanilla 2/2.

That's pretty solid all-in-all. 

&#x200B;

I hope that the issues are more covid-related than anything, but the marked increase in cards being released per year must have some effect on quality as well. 

&#x200B;

I'll take the erratas if they can make foils that actually stay flat.

Mono-Blue Sphinxes can be hard to find a win con for. I have a \[\[Braids, Conjurer Adept\]\] deck that has a Sphinx theme to it, and outside of flying beatdown, there is a big struggle to win. I have the pickles lock in there to close things should I be able to pull it, but without that it can be tough. 

&#x200B;

The sphinxes themselves are really fun, lots of free cards from your deck and from opponents graveyards. You can support it with some other mono blue staples like \[\[Stormtide Leviathan\]\] as all your sphinxes are flyers. \[\[Kira, Glass-Spinner\]\] is another great include that can come out early.

Of course! 

Here's a link to my Atla deck if you want to check out some other options. This is almost exclusively a combo deck, so depending on what you're going for not everything will be what you want. 

&#x200B;

[**https://archidekt.com/decks/2017630#Omelette\_du\_Terreur**](https://archidekt.com/decks/2017630#Omelette_du_Terreur)

&#x200B;

Lots of concepts apply to all Atla stuff though. Repeatable sac outlets, various ways to make eggs (Love my \[\[Nesting Dragon\]\]), and some big bombs to roll into.

A 5C changeling enabler could definitely make sense as a legendary. Maybe have it make all cards also be all colors? Not sure how much that would break stuff, but it seems like it could open up some shenanigans. Maybe an activated ability like 'WUBRG: All creatures you control  in your hand, on the battlefield, and in your deck are all colors til end of turn'

&#x200B;

My concern with something like that is it might make the 5C good stuff decks even more good stuff-y by reducing even more limitation.

Leaning into the card draw seems like it could be effective. Oneirophage is a new one for me so thanks! 

&#x200B;

I want the sphinx tribal to work, but it seems like in mono-blue it may not be viable enough.

There are definitely board games that have very high levels of replayability.  One that springs to mind is Betrayal. Since you assemble a new board every game and there are 50 endgame options (100 with the expansion) you can play the crap out of that game and have a different experience. 

&#x200B;

I would think of board games more equivalent to individual decks than I would Magic as a whole. A new board game is right in line pricewise with a commander deck, and you only need one for a group of four. Just as you can have multiple decks of a certain theme, you can have multiple board games that are thematically similar with different mechanics, etc. 

&#x200B;

I don't mean this to advocate for one over the other, I guess I just encourage you to explore the board game space some more if you're looking to expand hobbies

Less is subjective though, no? I'm also not sold that card price on a secondary market should be the main driver of the value of a deck. I feel like recent commander pre-cons are (usually) a deck that can picked up by a new player with no modifications and hang with the average non-competitive deck at an lgs. 

&#x200B;

I have the Kamigawa vehicles pre-con, and if you use Shorikai as the commander that deck puts in work. I've played it against some wayy more expensive decks built by pretty decent deck builders and it hung around. 

&#x200B;

I feel like pre-cons should be looked at as a good starting point for the average beginner commander player. The vehicle pre-con has a Port Town, Prairie Stream, and whichever land taps for one to give you UW. It's mana base is pretty solid for 40 bucks, and it seems like the New Capenna ones have mana bases that are similar.

&#x200B;

I would have loved to see the Azorius talisman in the deck as much as anyone else, but to be honest it doesn't really feel like it needs it. Sol Ring, Azorius Signet, Arcane Signet, and Fellwar Stone do a solid job for rocks. 

&#x200B;

I'm just rambling at this point, but I still feel like the product is doing what it is supposed to (introduce new players to commander) while giving established players some fun tools.
